  "verbalization": "Estriol is shown to have multiple targets that ultimately lead to the Liver X receptor (LXR alpha and LXR beta). The first pathway links Estriol to Ribosomal protein S6 kinase 2, which is targeted by Hydroxysaikosaponin c, and this compound in turn targets the LXR receptors. The second and third pathways connect Estriol to 11-cis retinol dehydrogenase, which is targeted by two different compounds—2-[(4S)-4-hydroxy-3,6-dioxodioxan-4-yl]acetic acid and 5-Alpha-Androstane-3-Beta,17beta-Diol—both of which also target the LXR receptors. These findings suggest that Estriol may influence the activity of the LXR pathway through multiple intermediate molecules. The evidence is consistent with a mechanism where Estriol modulates the LXR pathway via different downstream targets, potentially through metabolic or signaling pathways. The strength of the evidence is moderate, as multiple independent pathways converge on the LXR receptors, but the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated."
